+x, & x\\le 1, \\\\\nx+a, & 1<x<3, \\\\\nx^2-2x, & x\\ge 3.\n\\end{cases}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186235721 #edb/ref 17592186235292 "Consider the function $f(x)$, defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}\ne^x+x^2+x, & x\\le 1, \\\\\nx+a, & 1<x<3, \\\\\nx^2-2x, & x\\ge 3.\n\\end{cases}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186235737 #edb/ref 17592186235292 "Consider the function $f(x)$, defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}\ne^x+x^2+x, & x\\le 1, \\\\\nx+a, & 1<x<3, \\\\\nx^2-2x, & x\\ge 3.\n\\end{cases}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186235609 #edb/ref 17592186235293 "Maria and John are playing rock, paper, scissors. In each round, the players pick one of those three objects, and the rules are that rock beats scissors, scissors beats paper, and paper beats rock. \n\nThey decide to extend the game and make it so that the winner of each round receives $\\$2$ from the loser. So, for example, if Maria picks scissors and John picks rock, then Maria pays $\\$2$ to John.\n\nMaria represents the money she could earn or lose after one game using the following table.\n\n<div class=\"route-matrix route-matrix-wide\">\n  <span class=\"matrix-to\">John</span>\n  <span class=\"matrix-from\">Maria</span>\n  <table aria-label=\"Game payoffs: rows are Maria’s choices; columns are John’s choices\">\n    <thead>\n      <tr><td class=\"matrix-corner\"></td><th scope=\"col\">Rock</th><th scope=\"col\">Paper</th><th scope=\"col\">Scissors</th></tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Rock</th><td>$0$</td><td>$-2$</td><td>$2$</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Paper</th><td>$2$</td><td>$0$</td><td>$-2$</td></tr>\n<tr><th scope=\"row\">Scissors</th><td>$-2$</td><td>$2$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>"] [#edb/ref 17592186235642 #edb/ref 17592186235293 "Maria and John are playing rock, paper, scissors. In each round, the players pick one of those three objects, and the rules are that rock beats scissors, scissors beats paper, and paper beats rock. \n\nThey decide to exte