e equation\n\n$$\ny'=\\frac{\\sin(x)}{y^3}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186670633 #edb/ref 17592186670610 "Source question: Problem 1(a–c), question PDF page 1; key pages 1–2.\n\nConsider the equation\n\n$$\ny'=\\frac{\\sin(x)}{y^3}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186670604 #edb/ref 17592186670636 "Source question: Problem 2(a–b) and Theorem 2.3.1, question PDF page 2; key page 3.\n\n**Theorem 2.3.1 (provided in the source).**\n\n**(a)** If $f$ is continuous on an open rectangle\n\n$$\nR:\\{a<x<b,\\ c<y<d\\}\n$$\n\nthat contains $(x_0,y_0)$, then the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'=f(x,y),\\qquad y(x_0)=y_0 \\tag{2.3.1}\n$$\n\nhas at least one solution on some open subinterval of $(a,b)$ that contains $x_0$.\n\n**(b)** If both $f$ and $f_y$ are continuous on $R$, then (2.3.1) has a unique solution on some open subinterval of $(a,b)$ that contains $x_0$.\n\nConsider the initial value problems of the form\n\n$$\ny'=(x+y)^{1/3},\\qquad y(x_0)=y_0.\n$$\n\nHint: The function $h(s)=s^{1/3}$ is continuous for all $s$.\n\nYou do not need to identify all rectangles. However, to receive full credit, you must clearly identify $f(x,y)$ and $f_y(x,y)$ and state where each is continuous."] [#edb/ref 17592186670616 #edb/ref 17592186670636 "Source question: Problem 2(a–b) and Theorem 2.3.1, question PDF page 2; key page 3.\n\n**Theorem 2.3.1 (provided in the source).**\n\n**(a)** If $f$ is continuous on an open rectangle\n\n$$\nR:\\{a<x<b,\\ c<y<d\\}\n$$\n\nthat contains $(x_0,y_0)$, then the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'=f(x,y),\\qquad y(x_0)=y_0 \\tag{2.3.1}\n$$\n\nhas at least one solution on some open subinterval of $(a,b)$ that contains $x_0$.\n\n**(b)** If both $f$ and $f_y$ are continuous on $R$, then (2.3.1) has a unique solution on some open subinterval of $(a,b)$ that contains $x_0$.\n\nConsider the initial value problems of the form\n\n$$\ny'=(x+y)^{1/3},\\qquad y(x_0)=y_0.\n$$\n\nHint: The function $h(s)=s^{1/3}$ is continuous for all $s$.\n\nYou do not need to identify all rectangles. However, to receive full credit, you must clearly identify $f(x,y)$ and $f_y(x,y)$ and state where each is continuous."] [#edb/ref 17592186672082 #edb/ref 17592186672070 "Source question: Problem 5(a)–(e), PDF page 6.\n\n**Conservation of Energy.** In physics, use the potential convention $\\mathbf F=-\\nabla\\phi$, the negative of the usual mathematical convention. An object of constant mass $m$ moves in a conservative net force field $\\mathbf F$ along $\\mathbf r(t)$; write $\\mathbf v=\\mathbf r\\prime$. The source notes that Earth’s radial gravity field has exponent $p=3$ and is conservative by Problem 3. Follow the requested derivation step by step."] [#edb/ref 17592186672233 #edb/ref 17592186672070 "Source question: Problem 5(a)–(e), PDF page 6.\n\n**Conservation of Energy.** In physics, use the potential convention $\\mathbf F=-\\nabla\\phi$, the negative of the usual mathematical convention. An object of constant mass $m$ moves in a conservative net force field $\\mathbf F$ along $\\mathbf r(t)$; write $\\mathbf v=\\mathbf r\\prime$. The source notes that Earth’s radial gravity field has exponent $p=3$ and is conservative by Problem 3. Follow the requested derivation step by step."] [#edb/ref 17592186672309 #edb/ref 17592186672070 "Source question: Problem 5(a)–(e), PDF page 6.\n\n**Conservation of Energy.** In physics, use the potential convention $\\mathbf F=-\\nabla\\phi$, the negative of the usual mathematical convention. An object of constant mass $m$ moves in a conservative net force field $\\mathbf F$ along $\\mathbf r(t)$; write $\\mathbf v=\\mathbf r\\prime$. The source notes that Earth’s radial gravity field has exponent $p=3$ and is conservative by Problem 3. Follow the requested derivation step by step."] [#edb/ref 17592186672346 #edb/ref 17592186672070 "Source question: Problem 5(a)–(e), PDF page 6.\n\n**Conservation of Energy.** In physics, use the potential convention $\\mathbf F=-\\nabla\\phi$, the negative of the usual mathematical convention. An object of constant mass $m$ moves in a conservative net force field $\\mathbf F$ along $\\mathbf r(t)$; write $\\mathbf v=\\mathbf r\\prime$. The source notes that Earth’s radial gravity field has exponent $p=3$ and is conservative by Problem 3. Follow the requested derivation step by step."] [#edb/ref 17592186672397 #edb/ref 17592186672070 "Source question: Problem 5(a)–(e), PDF page 6.\n\n**Conservation of Energy.** In physics, use the potential convention $\\mathbf F=-\\nabla\\phi$, the negative of the usual mathematical convention. An object of constant mass $m$ moves in a conservative net force field $\\mathbf F$ along $\\mathbf r(t)$; write $\\mathbf v=\\mathbf r\\prime$. The source notes that Earth’s radial gravity field has exponent $p=3$ and is conservative by Problem 3. Follow the requested derivation step by step."] [#edb/ref 17592186672144 #edb/ref 17592186672092 "Source question: Problem 5(a)–(c), PDF page 6.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F=\\langle y,x\\rangle$, $O=(0,0)$, $A=(x,0)$, and $B=(x,y)$. Set $\\phi(O)=0$. Find a potential using the two specified line segments, rather than replacing the required method with direct partial integration."] [#edb/ref 17592186672234 #edb/ref 17592186672092 "Source question: Problem 5(a)–(c), PDF page 6.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F=\\langle y,x\\rangle$, $O=(0,0)$, $A=(x,0)$, and $B=(x,y)$. Set $\\phi(O)=0$. Find a potential using the two specified line segments, rather than replacing the required method with direct partial integration."] [#edb/ref 17592186672311 #edb/ref 17592186672092 "Source question: Problem 5(a)–(c), PDF page 6.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F=\\langle y,x\\rangle$, $O=(0,0)$, $A=(x,0)$, and $B=(x,y)$. Set $\\phi(O)=0$. Find a potential using the two specified line segments, rather than replacing the required method with direct partial integration."] [#edb/ref 17592186672141 #edb/ref 17592186672108 "Source question: [WeBWorK Problem 12](https://webwork2.science.oregonstate.edu/webwork2/MTH_256_020_F2026/WebWork_2/12).\